<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Rosario Murillo</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">b. <a href="/years/1951">1951</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Rosario Murillo is a Nicaraguan poet and politician, the wife of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daniel-ortega">Daniel Ortega</a> and his closest partner in power. Long the government's spokeswoman, she became vice-president in 2017 and, after a constitutional reform in 2025, co-president with Ortega. She is widely regarded as a principal architect of the repression of the 2018 protests and of the closure of independent media, universities and civil society groups.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rosario-murillo.jpg" alt="Rosario Murillo" width="292" height="372" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: 總統府 · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:2017_Rosario_Murillo_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Since then Ortega and his wife, <b>Rosario Murillo</b>, have dismantled checks on their power: protests in April 2018 were suppressed with more than 300 deaths, leading opposition candidates were jailed before the 2021 election, and hundreds of critics have been stripped of their citizenship.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-nicaragua">Republic of Nicaragua · Into the Present</a></li></ul></section>
